 'Those restless nights when sleep won''t come can feel overwhelming, filled with...''s peace can gently enter, reminding us that He is always near, even in our unrest. Psalm 4:8 assures us, ''In peace I will lie down and sleep, for you alone, Lord, make me dwell in safety.'' Trusting God doesn''t always make the anxiety vanish immediately, but it does provide a refuge when the night feels long.',
 'Tonight, before bed, try a simple breathing prayer: slowly inhale and say ''Lord, grant me peace,'' then exhale and say ''I trust You.'' Repeat this gently as you lie down, inviting God''s calming presence into your restless mind.',
 'What fears or worries tend to keep you awake, and how might inviting God into those moments shift your perspective?',
 'When sleep won''t come, it can feel like a...''In peace I will lie down and sleep, for you alone, Lord, make me dwell in safety.'' This isn''t a call to ignore our anxieties but an invitation to rest beneath the shelter of God''s care. Practically, this means turning our focus away from racing thoughts and towards God''s presence and promises. It''s okay to acknowledge the difficulty and the restlessness, but we can also choose to lean into faith as a source of peace. Tonight, if sleep evades you, try offering your worries to God through prayer or scripture, reminding yourself that His peace surpasses all understanding. In embracing this divine peace, we may find rest not only for our bodies but for our souls.',
